```
# ============================================================================
# Detection: New Autorun Registry Key or Startup Folder Entry
# Detection ID: DE-PE-001
# MITRE ATT&CK: T1547.001 - Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder
# Data Source: Sysmon EventCode=13 (RegistryEvent), EventCode=11 (FileCreate)
# Severity: Medium
# ============================================================================

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational" EventCode=13
TargetObject IN ("*\\CurrentVersion\\Run*","*\\CurrentVersion\\RunOnce*")
| stats count min(_time) as first_seen values(Details) as value_written by ComputerName, Image, TargetObject
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen,"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| append
    [ search index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational" EventCode=11
      TargetFilename="*\\Start Menu\\Programs\\Startup\\*"
      | stats count min(_time) as first_seen values(TargetFilename) as value_written by ComputerName, Image
      | eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen,"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"), TargetObject="StartupFolder" ]
| table first_seen, ComputerName, Image, TargetObject, value_written, count
| sort -first_seen
```

## Detection Logic
Combines registry Run/RunOnce key writes with Startup folder file drops — the two most common persistence mechanisms for commodity malware and simple implants.

## False Positive Considerations
Legitimate software installers write Run keys during setup. Correlate with a recent-install window (MSI events) to suppress installer-driven noise.

## Tuning Notes
Add EventCode=12 (RegistryEvent create/delete) to also catch key deletion, useful for detecting cleanup by attackers post-persistence-validation.
